body:
Trying to run vampire-parallel might cause the program to crash due to segmentation. The issue is not persistent, it does not always happen, and might not cause any problem on different machines.

Through search using debugging mode, with AddressSanitizer, two issues were found :

1. Mismatch of integer size in src/create/composition.cpp might have caused overflow in the parallel version of the program
In line 35 : 
    unsigned int num_atoms = 0;
Fix : 
    uint64_t num_atoms = 0;

2. Loop during the calculation of spin temperature in src/statistics/spin_temperature.cpp iterates through all atoms, not just the atoms owned that particular CPU, which causes the loop to iterate over the vector size.
In line 158 : 
    for(int atom=0; atom < num_atoms; ++atom)
Fix : 
    const int num_masked_atoms = mask.size();
    for(int atom =  0; atom < num_masked_atoms; ++atom)

Issue : Time is seen as not being incremented in output file when running field-cooling using spin-lattice, therefore causing the field not to be cooled.
Cause : Time is not incremented in src/simulate/sim.cpp

Root of problem :

`
case sim::suzuki_trotter: // spin-lattice Dynamics
        for(uint64_t ti=0;ti<n_steps;ti++){
        	sld::suzuki_trotter();
        }
        break;
`

Fix :

`
case sim::suzuki_trotter: // spin-lattice Dynamics
        for(uint64_t ti=0;ti<n_steps;ti++){
        	sld::suzuki_trotter();
        	// increment time
        	sim::internal::increment_time();
        }
        break;
`

When I tried to run field-cooling with spin-lattice integrator, it returns an error when output:spin-temperature is defined even though it is defined in the program. A small error in this part in src/vio/match.cpp :

test="material-spin-temperature";
if(word==test){
    stats::calculate_material_spin_temp = true;
    output_list.push_back(77);
}
Check status is not returned.

Fix :
Add return status as success.

test="material-spin-temperature";
if(word==test){
    stats::calculate_material_spin_temp = true;
    output_list.push_back(77);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

vdc --vtk copies the first spin "num_atoms" times, then it starts adding the remaining spins.
It seems the issue is from "vdc::atoms_list".
Replacing it by "sliced_atoms_list" on lines 69, 72 solves the issue.

```
- for(size_t i=0; i < vdc::atoms_list.size(); i++){
+ for(size_t i=0; i < vdc::sliced_atoms_list.size(); i++){
- unsigned int atom = vdc::atoms_list[i];
+ unsigned int atom = vdc::sliced_atoms_list[i];
```
